  ('f7abd074-e54c-4b2e-be75-0d930a4618cd', 'ba6d904f-c008-4013-904c-3f001e701055', NULL, 'discussion', 'Interesting paper

<!--QUOTED_POST_DATA:{"id":"e55aab7b-b132-42c6-af47-f008b3425409","content":"🧬 Discussion: AlphaFold — When AI Learned the Shape of Life\n\nFor decades, one of biology’s most difficult questions was deceptively simple:\n\nGiven a protein’s amino-acid sequence, what three-dimensional structure will it fold into?\n\nIn 2021, DeepMind’s AlphaFold demonstrated how far artificial intelligence could push this problem.\n\nAlphaFold uses deep-learning techniques to predict protein structures from their amino-acid sequences. Rather than explicitly simulating every physical interaction involved in protein folding, the model learns structural relationships from large amounts of biological data.\n\nThe result was a major advance in computational structural biology, with AlphaFold achieving highly accurate predictions for many proteins and dramatically expanding the scale at which researchers could investigate protein structure.\n\n🔬 Why does this matter?\n\nProtein structure is closely connected to biological function. Knowing the likely structure of a protein can help researchers investigate:\n\n• Protein function and molecular mechanisms\n• Disease-associated mutations\n• Protein–protein interactions\n• Drug discovery and molecular design\n• Protein engineering\n\nWhat makes AlphaFold particularly interesting is that it represents a shift in scientific computation:\n\nInstead of asking AI to imitate a human task, researchers used AI to attack a fundamental scientific problem.\n\nAnd that distinction matters.\n\nAlphaFold did not make experimental structural biology obsolete. Instead, it provided researchers with a powerful computational hypothesis-generation tool that can be combined with experimental approaches.\n\n📌 Scientific significance:\nAlphaFold helped establish AI-driven structure prediction as a major component of modern computational biology and demonstrated the potential of deep learning to accelerate scientific discovery.\n\n🔗 Paper: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-021-03819-2\n\n#AI #ArtificialIntelligence #AlphaFold #StructuralBiology #ComputationalBiology #DeepLearning #ProteinScience #DrugDiscovery #Science #Research #BOOFFIN","postType":"research_share","author":{"id":"e2e6bf3b-5c24-4c7f-b04d-b64cf365e730","fullName":"S S Mishra","handle":"revered_sage","avatarUrl":"https://lvstuqhrmagzqkgwlisl.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/profile-media/e2e6bf3b-5c24-4c7f-b04d-b64cf365e730/avatar_1791263628437.jpg?t=1791263630204","academicTitle":"In Training Student Researcher","institution":"AINN"},"createdAt":"Oct 1","paper":{"id":"557efdb8-38dc-4b80-b492-46dfb5efc24d","title":"Highly accurate protein structure prediction with AlphaFold","doi":"10.1038/s41586-021-03819-2","journal":"Nature"},"images":[]}-->', 'public', '{}', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2026-10-14T09:54:10.179521+00:00', '2026-10-14T09:54:10.179521+00:00'),
  ('88f0f199-af3e-4215-b08b-c6ae6b3c5d1a', '27611b22-0a90-449f-9b13-521ff0d6b7b4', NULL, 'discussion', 'insightfull

<!--QUOTED_POST_DATA:{"id":"cbd776ef-ad3f-472a-9d52-8eb3447da84e","content":"🧠 Discussion: Attention Is All You Need\n\nPublished in 2017, Attention Is All You Need introduced the Transformer, an architecture that fundamentally changed how neural networks process sequential information.\n\nAt its core is self-attention, a mechanism that allows a model to determine how different elements of a sequence relate to one another. Unlike earlier recurrent approaches, the Transformer can directly connect information across long distances within a sequence, without processing tokens strictly one after another.\n\nThe architecture introduced several key ideas:\n\n🔹 Self-Attention — enables each token to weigh the relevance of other tokens in the sequence.\n\n🔹 Multi-Head Attention — allows the model to learn different types of relationships simultaneously by processing attention through multiple representation subspaces.\n\n🔹 Positional Encoding — provides information about token order, allowing the model to process sequences in parallel while still retaining positional information.\n\n🔹 Parallelization — by removing recurrence from the core architecture, Transformers enabled substantially greater parallelism during training.\n\nThe authors demonstrated the architecture on machine translation, where the Transformer achieved strong results while requiring considerably less training time than several earlier sequence-modeling approaches.\n\nMore importantly, the paper established an architectural foundation that would later underpin much of modern natural language processing and large language models.\n\n📌 Why it matters:\nThe Transformer shifted the dominant paradigm from processing sequences step by step toward learning relationships across the sequence through attention. That seemingly simple change became one of the foundations of today''s generative AI.\n\n🔗 Original paper: https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762\n\n#ArtificialIntelligence #MachineLearning #DeepLearning #Transformers #NeuralNetworks #NLP #Research #AttentionIsAllYouNeed #BOOFFIN","postType":"research_share","author":{"id":"e2e6bf3b-5c24-4c7f-b04d-b64cf365e730","fullName":"S S Mishra","handle":"revered_sage","avatarUrl":"https://lvstuqhrmagzqkgwlisl.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/profile-media/e2e6bf3b-5c24-4c7f-b04d-b64cf365e730/avatar_1791263628437.jpg?t=1791263630204","academicTitle":"In Training Student Researcher","institution":"AINN"},"createdAt":"Oct 1","paper":{"id":"82288911-6964-4847-b726-1be21cb00afb","title":"Attention Is All You Need","journal":"arXiv Preprint"},"images":[]}-->', 'public', '{}', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2026-10-14T02:43:57.581706+00:00', '2026-10-14T02:43:57.581706+00:00'),
  ('cbd776ef-ad3f-472a-9d52-8eb3447da84e', 'e2e6bf3b-5c24-4c7f-b04d-b64cf365e730', '82288911-6964-4847-b726-1be21cb00afb', 'research_share', '🧠 Discussion: Attention Is All You Need

Published in 2017, Attention Is All You Need introduced the Transformer, an architecture that fundamentally changed how neural networks process sequential information.

At its core is self-attention, a mechanism that allows a model to determine how different elements of a sequence relate to one another. Unlike earlier recurrent approaches, the Transformer can directly connect information across long distances within a sequence, without processing tokens strictly one after another.

The architecture introduced several key ideas:

🔹 Self-Attention — enables each token to weigh the relevance of other tokens in the sequence.

🔹 Multi-Head Attention — allows the model to learn different types of relationships simultaneously by processing attention through multiple representation subspaces.

🔹 Positional Encoding — provides information about token order, allowing the model to process sequences in parallel while still retaining positional information.

🔹 Parallelization — by removing recurrence from the core architecture, Transformers enabled substantially greater parallelism during training.

The authors demonstrated the architecture on machine translation, where the Transformer achieved strong results while requiring considerably less training time than several earlier sequence-modeling approaches.

More importantly, the paper established an architectural foundation that would later underpin much of modern natural language processing and large language models.

📌 Why it matters:
The Transformer shifted the dominant paradigm from processing sequences step by step toward learning relationships across the sequence through attention. That seemingly simple change became one of the foundations of today''s generative AI.

🔗 Original paper: https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762

